      "text": "To solve for the largest \\(t\\) value at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees, we start by setting the temperature equation equal to 77:\n\n\\[\n-t^2 + 12t + 50 = 77\n\\]\n\nSubtract 77 from both sides to set the problem to zero:\n\n\\[\n-t^2 + 12t + 50 - 77 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n-t^2 + 12t - 27 = 0\n\\]\n\nMultiply both sides by -1 to make the coefficient of \\(t^2\\) positive:\n\n\\[\nt^2 - 12t + 27 = 0\n\\]\n\nNow, we solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(t = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\). Here, \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -12\\), and \\(c = 27\\).\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{(-12)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 27}}{2 \\cdot 1}\n\\]\n\nCalculate the discriminant:\n\n\\[\n(-12)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 27 = 144 - 108 = 36\n\\]\n\nNow, apply the quadratic formula:\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{36}}{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sqrt{36} = 6\\), we have:\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{12 \\pm 6}{2}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{12 + 6}{2} = \\frac{18}{2} = 9 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad t = \\frac{12 - 6}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the largest \\(t\\) value for which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]",
