      "text": "To determine the time $t$ when the temperature is exactly 77 degrees, we need to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n-t^2 + 12t + 50 = 77\n$$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the equation into standard quadratic form:\n\n$$\n-t^2 + 12t - 27 = 0\n$$\n\nNow, multiply through by $-1$ to make the leading coefficient positive:\n\n$$\nt^2 - 12t + 27 = 0\n$$\n\nWe will solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula, which is given by:\n\n$$\nt = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nHere, $a = 1$, $b = -12$, and $c = 27$. Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\n$$\nt = \\frac{-(-12) \\pm \\sqrt{(-12)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 27}}{2 \\cdot 1} \\\\\nt = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{144 - 108}}{2} \\\\\nt = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{36}}{2} \\\\\nt = \\frac{12 \\pm 6}{2}\n$$\n\nThe two possible solutions for $t$ are:\n\n$$\nt = \\frac{12 + 6}{2} = 9 \\\\\nt = \\frac{12 - 6}{2} = 3\n$$\n\nThus, the temperature reaches 77 degrees at $t = 9$ hours or $t = 3$ hours after noon. Since we are asked for the largest $t$ value at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees, the answer is $\\boxed{9}$.",
