      "text": "To find the largest \\( t \\) value at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees, we start with the given equation for temperature:\n\n\\[\n-T^2 + 12T + 50 = 77\n\\]\n\nFirst, set the equation to 77:\n\n\\[\n-T^2 + 12T + 50 = 77\n\\]\n\nNext, move all terms to one side of the equation to set it to zero:\n\n\\[\n-T^2 + 12T + 50 - 77 = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplify the equation:\n\n\\[\n-T^2 + 12T - 27 = 0\n\\]\n\nMultiply through by -1 to make the leading coefficient of \\( T^2 \\) positive:\n\n\\[\nT^2 - 12T + 27 = 0\n\\]\n\nNow, solve the quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n\\[\nT = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n\\]\n\nHere, \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -12 \\), and \\( c = 27 \\). Plug these values into the quadratic formula:\n\n\\[\nT = \\frac{-(-12) \\pm \\sqrt{(-12)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 27}}{2 \\cdot 1}\n\\]\n\nSimplify inside the square root:\n\n\\[\nT = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{144 - 108}}{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nT = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{36}}{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nT = \\frac{12 \\pm 6}{2}\n\\]\n\nThis gives two solutions:\n\n\\[\nT = \\frac{12 + 6}{2} = 9\n\\]\n\n\\[\nT = \\frac{12 - 6}{2} = 3\n\\]\n\nSince \\( t \\) represents time in hours past noon, the largest time at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]",
